<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-subspaces" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Subspaces</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Some subsets of <m>\R^n</m> are closed under the two operations we care about:
      adding two of their vectors, or scaling one, never takes you outside the subset.
      These are the subspaces, and they turn out to be exactly the spans.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-subspace-definition">
    <title>Definition and Examples</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-subspace">
      <title>Subspace</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>H</m> be a <em>nonempty</em> collection of vectors in <m>\R^n</m>. Then
          <m>H</m> is a <term>subspace</term> if whenever <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are
          scalars and <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> are vectors in <m>H</m>,
          <m>a\vec{u} + b\vec{v}</m> is also in <m>H</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="eg-subspace-or-not">
      <title>Which of These Is a Subspace?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Among the collection of vectors described below, which one is a subspace of
          <m>\R^n</m>?
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>A plane through the origin in <m>\R^3</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>A line not containing the origin in <m>\R^2</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <term>(A) Yes, this is a subspace.</term>
          <ol>
            <li><p>This subset of <m>\R^3</m> contains the zero vector.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                If two vectors are on this plane, so is any linear combination of them.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(B) No, this is not a subspace.</term>
          Note that, for example, <m>\vec{u}</m> is in the subset of vectors drawn along
          the line, however <m>-\vec{u}</m> is not. Hence this subset of vectors in
          <m>\R^2</m> is NOT a subspace of <m>\R^2</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <figure xml:id="fig-plane-subspace">
      <caption>
        A plane through the origin in <m>\R^3</m> is closed under linear combinations: if
        <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> lie in <m>H</m>, so does
        <m>a\vec{u} + b\vec{v}</m>.
      </caption>
      <image width="58%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1, every node/.style={font=\small}]
            \fill[blue!8] (-1.6,-0.9) -- (3.4,-0.9) -- (4.6,1.3) -- (-0.4,1.3) -- cycle;
            \draw[blue!45] (-1.6,-0.9) -- (3.4,-0.9) -- (4.6,1.3) -- (-0.4,1.3) -- cycle;
            \coordinate (O) at (0.9,0.05);
            \draw[green!55!black, very thick, -{Stealth[length=2.2mm]}] (O) -- ++(0.55,0.85)
              node[above left, black] {$\vec{v}$};
            \draw[red, very thick, -{Stealth[length=2.2mm]}] (O) -- ++(1.9,-0.15)
              node[below right, black] {$\vec{u}$};
            \draw[violet, very thick, -{Stealth[length=2.2mm]}] (O) -- ++(2.45,0.7)
              node[above right, black] {$a\vec{u}+b\vec{v}$};
            \node at (0.1,-0.62) {$H$};
            \node at (2.0,1.05) {$ax+by+cz=0$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-line-not-subspace">
      <caption>
        A line in <m>\R^2</m> that misses the origin is not a subspace: <m>\vec{u}</m>
        lies on the line but <m>-\vec{u}</m> does not.
      </caption>
      <image width="50%">
        <prefigure label="pf-line-not-subspace" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
          <diagram dimensions="(300,280)" margins="10">
            <coordinates bbox="(-2.6,-2.4,2.8,2.4)">
              <axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" arrows="2" decorations="no"/>
              <line endpoints="((-2.6,0.2),(2.6,2.0))" stroke="red" thickness="2"/>
              <line endpoints="((0,0),(1.4,1.58))" stroke="green" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
              <line endpoints="((0,0),(-1.4,-1.58))" stroke="magenta" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
              <label p="(1.4,1.58)" alignment="e" offset="(6,2)" color="green"><m>\vec{u}</m></label>
              <label p="(-1.4,-1.58)" alignment="w" offset="(-6,-2)" color="magenta"><m>-\vec{u}</m></label>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <example xml:id="eg-subspace-contains-zero">
      <title>True or False?</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Any subspace <m>H</m> of <m>\R^n</m> contains the zero vector.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          This is true. Note that since a subspace is nonempty, it has at least one
          element <m>\vec{u}</m>. But then <m>0\vec{u} = \vec{0}</m> is in <m>H</m> since
          <m>H</m> is closed under scalar multiplication.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-not-subspace-affine">
      <title>A Set That Misses the Origin</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is the set
          <m>T = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} x - 1 \\ x + 1 \end{bmatrix}, \; x \in \R \right\}</m>
          a subspace of <m>\R^2</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          No. Clearly <m>\vec{0} \notin T</m>, since
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} x-1 \\ x+1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>
          is an inconsistent system: it would need <m>x = 1</m> and <m>x = -1</m> at once.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-subspace-line">
      <title>A Line through the Origin Is a Subspace</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is the set
          <m>T = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} t \; : \; t \in \R \right\}</m>
          a subspace of <m>\R^3</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Yes.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(I)</term> Choose <m>t = 0</m> to see that <m>\vec{0} \in T</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <term>(II)</term> For any <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> in <m>T</m>,
          <m>a\vec{u} + b\vec{v}</m> is also in <m>T</m>. If
          <m>\vec{u} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} t_1</m> and
          <m>\vec{v} = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} t_2</m>, then
          <md>
            <mrow>
              a\vec{u} + b\vec{v}
              \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} a t_1
                   + \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} b t_2
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} \left( a t_1 + b t_2 \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \amp = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ -1 \end{bmatrix} t_3,
                \qquad \text{where } t_3 \in \R.
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="eg-subspaces-of-r3">
      <title>Examples of Subspaces of <m>\R^3</m></title>
      <p>
        <term>(I)</term>
        <m>H_1 = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix} \in \R^3 : x = y = z \right\}</m>.
        Note that this is a line that passes through the origin and can be thought of as
        <m>H_1 = \operatorname{Span}\left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>(II)</term>
        <m>H_2 = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix} \in \R^3 : x - y = 0 \right\}</m>.
        Note that this is a plane that passes through the origin and can be thought of as
        <m>H_2 = \operatorname{Span}\left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>(III)</term>
        <md>
          H_3 = \R^3
          = \operatorname{Span}\left\{
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
            \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
            \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\}
          = \operatorname{Span}\left\{
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
            \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
            \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \right\},
        </md>
        since
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{bmatrix}
          = \left( \frac{x+y}{2} \right) \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
          + \left( \frac{x-y}{2} \right) \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}
          + z \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-subspaces-are-spans">
    <title>Subspaces Are Spans</title>

    <theorem xml:id="thm-subspaces-are-spans">
      <title>Subspaces Are Spans and Spans Are Subspaces</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>H</m> be a nonempty collection of vectors in <m>\R^n</m>. Then <m>H</m>
          is a subspace of <m>\R^n</m> if and only if there exist vectors
          <m>\{ \vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_k \}</m> in <m>H</m> such that
          <md>
            H = \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_k \}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          We only show that if <m>H</m> is a subspace, then
          <m>H = \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_k \}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Pick a vector <m>\vec{u}_1 \in H</m>. If
          <m>H = \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}_1 \}</m>, then we are done. If
          <m>H \neq \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}_1 \}</m>, then there exists another
          vector <m>\vec{u}_2 \in H</m> which is not in
          <m>\operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}_1 \}</m>. Now consider
          <m>\operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}_1, \vec{u}_2 \}</m>. If
          <m>H = \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}_1, \vec{u}_2 \}</m>, then we are done.
          Otherwise, pick
          <m>\vec{u}_3 \notin \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}_1, \vec{u}_2 \}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          If we continue this way, the process must stop with <m>\vec{u}_k</m>, where
          <m>k \leq n</m>, and thus
          <m>H = \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_k \}</m>. The process
          must terminate because, as we discussed in
          <xref ref="thm-too-many-vectors" text="type-global" />, in <m>\R^n</m>, if
          <m>k \gt n</m> then the set <m>\{ \vec{u}_1, \vec{u}_2, \ldots, \vec{u}_k \}</m>
          is linearly dependent.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </theorem>

    <corollary xml:id="cor-subspaces-independent-spans">
      <title>Subspaces Are Spans of Linearly Independent Vectors</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>H</m> is a subspace of <m>\R^n</m>, then there exist linearly independent
          vectors <m>\{ \vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_k \}</m> of <m>H</m> such that
          <md>
            H = \operatorname{Span}\{ \vec{u}_1, \ldots, \vec{u}_k \}.
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Since the vectors <m>\vec{u}_i</m> we picked in the proof above are not in the
          span of the previous vectors, they must be linearly independent.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </corollary>

    <example xml:id="eg-subspace-of-r3-parametrized">
      <title>Recognizing a Subspace from a Parametrization</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is the set
          <m>V = \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} a - 3b \\ 2a + b \\ a \end{bmatrix}, \; a, b \in \R \right\}</m>
          a subspace of <m>\R^3</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Yes, since
          <md>
            \begin{bmatrix} a - 3b \\ 2a + b \\ a \end{bmatrix}
            = a \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
            + b \begin{bmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
          </md>
          which means
          <m>V = \operatorname{Span}\left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Hence, from <xref ref="thm-subspaces-are-spans" text="type-global" />, we can
          conclude that <m>V</m> is a subspace of <m>\R^3</m>. The set
          <m>\left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} \right\}</m>
          is a linearly independent spanning set for <m>V</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Note that <m>V</m> can also be written as
          <md>
            V = \operatorname{Span}\left\{
              \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix},
              \begin{bmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix},
              \begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} \right\},
          </md>
          where this spanning set is <em>not</em> linearly independent, since
          <m>\begin{bmatrix} -2 \\ 3 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
          = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} + \begin{bmatrix} -3 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
